{"id": "66d655dc-8e67-41ee-abed-165a3023510b", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Since the user explicitly asked to triage this in chat rather than go through a formal investigation, I should proceed with exactly one background subagent call preloaded with the full investigation skill set, using a deep research mode prompt, and let the user know what I'm doing first.

I'm planning to preload skills for log searching, metrics, traces, infrastructure changes, deployments, and topology discovery, since we need the subagent to figure out where things run and distinguish between provider-side issues (like 429 rate limits or 5xx errors) versus network-level problems (NAT gateway errors, timeouts, security group or NACL changes). I'll scope the investigation from this morning onward with yesterday as baseline, and keep my message to the user brief before making the call.

"}]}

{"id": "e955ff59-a83b-47af-ab8f-b508d524f8e7", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "I'll triage this from what's visible in this account. I'll look at the failing calls' error types and status codes, the network path out to OpenAI (NAT, DNS, security groups), and recent deploys or config changes. Then I'll compare the failure start time against your change timeline. This is a deeper pass, so it may take a few minutes.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_VaKCft3FvfnHVMr3vB6U5c", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "openai-failure-triage", "agent_type": "generic", "preloaded_skills": ["searching-logs", "exploring-metrics", "exploring-traces", "investigating-infrastructure-changes", "discovering-deployments", "discovering-topology", "verifying-with-docs"], "prompt": "[DEEP RESEARCH MODE]
{
  "task": "Triage whether a ~33% failure rate on outbound calls to the OpenAI API (direct, NOT Bedrock) is caused by the provider (OpenAI) or by the customer's own side (their app, network egress, config, quota/rate limits). Produce an evidence-based verdict with confidence level and clearly separate facts from hypotheses.",
  "context": "Customer's AI agent calls the OpenAI API directly for inference. Since the morning of 2026-11-24 (UTC; current time 2026-11-24T18:56Z) about one third of calls fail. Customer's own application metrics look normal (so failures may not be surfacing in their dashboards, or are retried/swallowed). We do NOT know where the agent runs (could be Lambda, ECS/EKS, EC2, etc.) - discover it from topology, associations, and log groups in agent space account 012345678901_1 (us-east-1 primary; check other regions if the workload is found there, and any other associated accounts). We cannot query OpenAI's status page or OpenAI-side data; only AWS/associated tooling. Be explicit about this limitation.",
  "resources": ["AWS account 012345678901_1", "region us-east-1 (check others if workload found elsewhere)", "agent/inference workload - discover via log groups, Lambda/ECS/EKS/EC2, API calls to api.openai.com"],
  "timeframe": "Failures start 'this morning' on 2026-11-24 - find the precise onset (UTC) from data. Incident window: 2026-11-24T00:00:00Z to 2026-11-24T18:56:00Z. Baseline: 2026-11-23T00:00:00Z to 2026-11-24T00:00:00Z (and prior days if needed).",
  "criteria": "1) Locate the agent workload and its logs. Find the failing calls and classify errors: HTTP status (429 rate limit / insufficient_quota, 500/502/503/529 overloaded, 401/403 auth, 400 request errors e.g. context length/model deprecated), vs. client-side timeouts, connection resets, DNS failures, TLS errors. Quantify counts/rates over time vs baseline, per model/endpoint/instance/AZ if available. Check whether failures are uniform across instances/AZs/subnets/NAT gateways (uniform -> provider or account/key-level; localized -> our network/infra) and whether they cluster by model, request size, or API key. Look for OpenAI response headers/error bodies in logs (x-request-id, retry-after, x-ratelimit-*). 2) Check egress path health: NAT Gateway metrics (ErrorPortAllocation, PacketsDropCount, ConnectionAttemptCount vs EstablishedCount, IdleTimeoutCount), VPC flow log rejects if available, Route 53 Resolver/DNS errors, proxy/firewall, security group/NACL/route table state. 3) Look for changes in our environment around the onset: deployments, config/env var/secret changes (API key rotation, model name change, SDK version bump), Lambda/ECS/EKS config changes, scaling events, increased traffic/concurrency (which could cause us to hit OpenAI rate limits), retry-storm behavior. Use CloudTrail via the proper skill tool and Kubernetes events if EKS. 4) Check traces (X-Ray) for latency/error on the OpenAI dependency span if present. 5) Explain why customer's own metrics look normal (e.g. errors caught and retried, success counted at wrapper level, failures logged but not metricized). 6) Final output: verdict (provider-side / our-side / inconclusive) with confidence, the key evidence with timestamps and numbers, what you could NOT verify (e.g. OpenAI-side status, org-level rate-limit dashboard), and the 2-3 most useful next checks for the customer (e.g. check OpenAI status page, usage/limits dashboard, x-request-ids to give OpenAI support). Do not state a cause unless data supports it; label hypotheses as hypotheses. Read-only: make no mutating calls."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Triage OpenAI call failures: logs, network path, recent changes"}], "created_at": null}

{"id": "27c44cf4-cf82-4644-8772-889bc88c5b98", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The billing agent shows zero records scanned in the 2-day window, suggesting it's inactive. I should check which log groups actually have recent activity—since describe_log_groups doesn't expose last event timestamps, I'll look at describe_log_streams ordered by last event time for a few of the larger groups instead.

Actually, a faster approach might be checking CloudWatch Lambda metrics directly: querying Invocations summed by function name across the window to see which functions are actually active.

"}], "parent_id": "401297ec-ab46-4ed8-9e69-d8e422285b3e"}

